.TH MIRACAST 1 "November 2026" "miracast 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
miracast \- Miracast (Wi-Fi Display) source and sink for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B miracast scan
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B miracast cast
[\fIname\fR|\fIIP\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B miracast sink
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B miracast
sends the screen and the system audio of this computer to a Miracast
receiver (typically a smart TV), or receives a Miracast stream and displays it.
.PP
Two transports are supported:
.TP
.B Miracast over Infrastructure (MS-MICE)
The source and the sink talk over the existing IP network (Wi-Fi or
Ethernet).
The sink is found by mDNS (service
.IR _display._tcp ),
the source connects to TCP port 7250 and the sink then connects back to the
RTSP port 7236.
No special Wi-Fi hardware is needed, but the sink must implement MS-MICE.
.TP
.B Wi-Fi Direct (P2P)
The classic Miracast transport.
It is driven through
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
and needs a Wi-Fi adapter whose driver exposes the P2P-client/P2P-GO modes
(see
.BR "iw phy" ).
.PP
In both cases the session is the Wi-Fi Display RTSP exchange (messages M1 to M7,
keep-alive, IDR request and teardown), and the media is H.264 video (optionally
AAC-LC audio) in an MPEG-2 transport stream carried over RTP, produced and
consumed through GStreamer.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B scan
List the sinks found nearby: first those announcing themselves on the network
by mDNS, then Wi-Fi Direct peers.
Each line has three tab-separated fields: address, transport
.RI ( network ,
.IR wifi-direct-sink ,
.IR wifi-direct-source ),
and name.
.TP
.BR cast " [\fIname\fR|\fIIP\fR]"
Send the screen and audio to a sink.
With a name, the sink is looked up by mDNS; with an IPv4 address it is used
directly; with no argument the only sink on the network is used.
If no sink is found on the network, Wi-Fi Direct is tried (the argument is then
the device name or MAC address).
Stop with
.BR Ctrl-C .
.TP
.B sink
Receive a stream and display it.
Over Wi-Fi Direct the sink waits for a source and becomes the group owner
(serving DHCP with
.BR dnsmasq (8)).
With
.B \-\-no\-p2p
it listens on an IP address.
.SH LINK OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interface " \fIIF\fR"
Wireless interface that wpa_supplicant uses for Wi-Fi Direct (default
.IR wlan0 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-ctrl\-dir " DIR"
wpa_supplicant control socket directory (default
.IR /run/wpa_supplicant ).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " \fINAME\fR"
Device name advertised to peers (default
.IR miracast-linux ).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-scan\-time " \fISEC\fR"
Wi-Fi Direct scan duration (default 8).
.TP
.B \-\-mice
(cast) Force Miracast over Infrastructure to the address given with
.BR \-\-host .
.TP
.B \-\-p2p
(cast) Skip the network discovery and use Wi-Fi Direct.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-p2p
Never use Wi-Fi Direct; use an existing IP network.
.TP
.BI \-\-host " IP"
(cast) Sink address, with
.B \-\-mice
or
.BR \-\-no\-p2p .
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " IP"
(sink) Address to bind the RTSP port to, with
.B \-\-no\-p2p
(default 0.0.0.0).
.SH SOURCE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-output " NAME"
Monitor to share, such as
.IR eDP-1 .
Default: the compositor's choice.
.TP
.BI \-\-capture " FRAGMENT"
GStreamer source fragment to capture instead of the screen, for example
.IR "pipewiresrc path=42" .
.TP
.B \-\-test\-pattern
Stream a moving test pattern instead of the screen.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-width " PX"
Largest picture width to negotiate (default 1920).
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-fps " N"
Highest frame rate to negotiate (default 60).
.TP
.BI \-\-bitrate " KBPS"
Video bit rate in kilobits per second (default 8000).
.TP
.BI \-\-encoder " NAME"
H.264 encoder element:
.I x264enc
(CPU, default),
.I vah264enc
(GPU through VA-API), or any other GStreamer H.264 encoder.
.TP
.BI \-\-encoder\-opts " PROPERTIES"
Extra element properties appended to the encoder, for example
.IR "speed-preset=veryfast" .
.TP
.BI \-\-buffer\-ms " N"
Length in milliseconds of the queue between capture and encoder; 0 disables it
(default 100).
Smaller values lower the latency.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-audio
Do not send audio.
By default the system output is captured and sent as AAC-LC, 48\ kHz, stereo,
when the sink supports it.
.TP
.BI \-\-audio\-source " FRAGMENT"
GStreamer audio source fragment to use instead of the system output.
.SH SINK OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-jitter\-ms " N"
Receive jitter buffer in milliseconds (default 40).
.TP
.BI \-\-video\-sink " ELEMENT"
GStreamer video sink (default
.IR autovideosink ).
.TP
.BI \-\-audio\-sink " ELEMENT"
GStreamer audio sink (default
.IR autoaudiosink ).
.SH GENERAL OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More logging; give it twice for a protocol trace.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Find sinks and cast to the only one on the network:
.PP
.RS
.nf
miracast scan --no-p2p
miracast cast
.fi
.RE
.PP
Share one monitor with the GPU encoder at 720p30:
.PP
.RS
.nf
miracast cast la-tevezita --output eDP-1 --encoder vah264enc \e
        --max-width 1280 --max-fps 30
.fi
.RE
.PP
Receive a stream on this machine, over the local network:
.PP
.RS
.nf
miracast sink --no-p2p --listen 0.0.0.0
.fi
.RE
.SH REQUIREMENTS
GStreamer base, good, bad, ugly and libav plugins, plus
.I gst-plugin-va
for GPU encoding and for importing the DMA-BUF frames that some compositors
(for example niri) deliver.
Screen capture uses the
.I org.gnome.Mutter.ScreenCast
D-Bus API and PipeWire.
Wi-Fi Direct additionally needs root (or CAP_NET_ADMIN), a wpa_supplicant built
with P2P and Wi-Fi Display support, a P2P-capable driver, a DHCP client and
.BR dnsmasq (8).
.SH EXIT STATUS
The values of
.BR sysexits.h (3):
0 success; 64 usage error; 68 sink not found; 69 service unavailable (for
example no P2P support, or the sink refuses the connection); 70 protocol
error; 71 operating-system error; 74 I/O error; 75 temporary failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wpa_supplicant (8),
.BR gst-launch-1.0 (1),
.BR iw (8),
.BR dnsmasq (8)
.PP
Wi-Fi Display Technical Specification v2.1 (Wi-Fi Alliance) and
[MS-MICE]: Miracast over Infrastructure Connection Establishment Protocol
(Microsoft).
.SH BUGS
Stream encryption (HDCP, DTLS) and PIN entry of MS-MICE are not implemented.
The implementation follows the public specifications and has not been certified
by the Wi-Fi Alliance.
